XSCEN - the scenario compiler.
X
XA scenario is composed of records that define objects, locations and
Xitems are two examples of objects in the game.
X
XA typical location record might be:
X
Xl 76		; Location 76, the passage outside your cabin
X%"in the passage outside your cabin"
X[The ground here is rather messy, it shows signs of the heavy
Xtraffic that is usually passing back and forth]
X,
Xsd42
Xus15
Xnx44
Xwp73
Xen76
Xc10
X.
X
XThe l line starts a location record, and is followed by the number of the
Xlocation.
X
XA '%' line is needed to contain a description, this will be printed
Xevery time you are at the location. This can be up to 250 characters.
XThe description should be enclosed in '"'s, and can extend over multiple
Xlines. As an alternative, if you wish two locations to have the same
Xdescription, then enter the description for the first of the locations
Xdefined, and in the subsequent ones, enter the number of the location
Xwhose description you want to copy, so:
X
Xl 77
X% 76
X
X.....
X
Xwould make location 77 have the same description as location 76. Note that
Xthis will only work if location 76 has already been defined: forward
Xreferences don't work.
X
XA '[' or '{' field can contain a longer description. If it is started
Xwith a '[', it should be terminated with a ']', and if started with a
X'{', end with a '}'. This can be up to 4090 characters in length. If
X'[' and ']' are used, the description will be printed the first time
Xthe player arrives at the location, and whenever the look command is
Xissued. If '{' and '}' are use, the long description will only be given
Xin response to a look command.
X
XA ',' specifies that this location is dark. Item 1 is special: it is
Xconsidered the "lamp" item. If the player has item 1 in his inventory,
Xthen the commands 'on' and 'off' will activate it. If the lamp is
Xon, in the players inventory or at the same location as the player,
Xthen the player will be able to see.
X
Xn s e w u d fields specify the six directions. Following the direction
Xshould be a second letter to say what sort of route exists. p is a
Xpassage, d is a door, s is stairs, and x is a way that exists without
Xbeing described. After the route description is the number of the
Xlocation at the other end of the route. If no field is entered for a
Xgiven direction, movement that way will generate a 'You can't go that way'
Xmessage. As an expansion of this, route code 'n' is a blocked way. This
Xmust be followed by a string number, and when the player tries to go
Xthat way, his passage is blocked, and the string is printed. Note that
Xthis string is taken from the standard string table (like item descriptions
Xetc.) it is only location descriptions that have a separate table.
X
XSo:
X
Xsd42		; door south to location 42
Xus15		; stairs up to 15
Xnx44		; way north to 15, with no description
Xwp73		; passage west to 73
Xen76		; no way east, but print string 76 when he tries it
X		; and no way down at all.
X
XA 'c' field will specify a score for reaching the location, the first
Xtime the player reaches this location, he gets that many points.
X
XFinally, the location record must be terminated by a '.'
X
XLocation numbers run from 1 to 199, and certain locations have special
Xproperties. Location 1 is where the player will start. Locations 2 through
X10 are 'hide rooms', the hide capability of items will cause them not to
Xshow in these rooms. Locations 5 through 15 are 'safe rooms', if a guard
Xis following you it won't go into one of these rooms, but will re-appear
Xwhen you exit. Also note that in effect records (described below) locations
Xcan be used for various purposes, but only locations 1 through 99 can
Xbe used here.
X
X
X
XItem records:
X
Xi 15
X%"A diamond ring"
Xx73
X'diamond
X'ring
Xh
Xa
Xc 5
Xl 2
Xw 15
X.
X
Xis a typical item.
X
XThe first line consists of an 'i' and the item number.
X
XThe '%' line is the item's description, this can either be a string (as shown)
Xor a string number. Note that unlike the descriptions of locations, these
Xnumbers are actual string numbers, as defined in the string table
X
XThe 'x' line is optional, and gives a string to be printed when the
Xobject is examined. Again this can be defined in line, or given by number.
X
XThe 'word lines define two words by which this object can be referred, these
Xare used when the object is examined, taken or dropped.
X
XThe 'h' specifies that when this is at one of the "hide" locations (2 - 10)
Xit does not normally show in the list of items seen. However, a search
Xcommand will always show it.
X
XThe 'a' specifies that a 'take all' should not include this object.
X
XThe 'c' gives a score that is gained the first time this is picked up.
X
XThe 'l' is the starting location of the object. Note that this can be
Xa standard location (1 - 199), and there are also three special cases:
X-1 is in the players inventory, -2 is "out of the game", i.e. at no
Xaccessible place, and -3 is a synonym for wherever the player is.
X
XThe 'w' is the weight of the object: this simply limits what the player
Xcan pick up, he has a maximum carrying capacity of 100. There are a
Xmaximum of 59 items, but only items 1 through 44 can ever appear in the
Xplayers inventory, and these are the only ones that show up on the ground,
Xand can be taken. 45 through 59 are reserved as a "holding place" for
Xitems that are to be copied, this is covered in the effects section.
X
X
X
Xa Mr. Spock
X
XThere is an option for an assistant in this game, if the assistant becomes
Xenabled, then he/she/it will follow you around. The 'a' record provides the
Xdescription of the assistant. The description should be no more than 39
Xcharacters.
X
X
X
Xg 8 10 12 There is a Klingon guard here!
X
XThere is also an option for random guard encounters: the 'g' record will
Xdefine how these work. Firstly there are three numbers: the number of
Xguards, the probability of one appearing, the action taken when one is
Xpresent. So in this case, a maximum of 8 guards would appear during the
Xgame, on each move (when they are enabled) there would be a 10% chance of
Xone appearing, and when there is one present, effect 12 would be activated
Xonce per turn. The remaining text is printed when there is one present,
Xand should be limited to 59 characters.
X
X
Xx 300
X
XThe 'x' record simply shows the maximum score that can be attained, for
Xuse in response to the sc command.
X
X
X
Xm 10 20 30
X
X'm' records allow effects to be attached to moving between locations.
XWhenever the player moves, the locations he's moving from and to are
Xcompared to all 'm' records, and the associated effect is done for any
Xthat match. So using the above example, movement from location 10 to
Xlocation 20 would cause effect 30 to be activated. The from and to
Xlocations may be entered as -1, which corresponds to any location, so:
X
Xm -1 15 76
X
Xwould activate effect 76 any time the player arrived at location 15.
XNote that a 'wait' command is considered a move from the current location
Xto itself, so that if a wait command were done at location 15, the above
X'm' record would match.
X
XAs a special case:
X
Xm -1 -1 42
X
Xwill activate for any move or wait command, since both the from and to
Xlocations are -1.
X
X
X
Xp 'verb 'noun 54
X
X'p' records define action phrases: when the command 'verb noun' is entered,
Xaction 54 would be executed. This can also be a single imperative:
X
Xp 'wave 13
X
Xwould activate effect 13 in response to the command 'wave'
X
XIt should be noted that only the first four letters of words are considered
Ximportant, further ones are ignored, so: tarpit and tarpaulin would be
Xconsidered the same word: tarp
X
X
X
XEffect records are the game's way of doing things. An effect record starts
Xwith a line:
X
Xf 23
X
Xthat defines this as effect 23, and ends with a single '.' on a line.
XBetween these can be an arbitrary number of 'if / then' lines, that
Xtest conditions, and perform actions if a set of conditions matches.
X
Xf 17
Xif ( conditions )
X  then ( actions )
Xif ( other conditions )
X  then ( other actions )
Xelse
X  then ( last actions )
X.
X
XThe above shows a typical effect record. The first set of conditions are
Xtested, if they are true, then the first set of actions are performed,
Xand then processing stops (with one exception, noted below). If the
Xconditions fail, then the next set of conditions are tried, if they are
Xtrue then their actions are done. The else is optional, and equates to
Xan 'if ( true )' and will act as a catch-all if none of the other
Xconditions match.
X
XIn any set of conditions, the whole set is considered true only if all the
Xindividual conditions evaluate true.
X
X
XThe tests that can be done are:
X
Xvx=n		Tests if variable x is equal to n
Xvx#n		................... is not equal to n
Xvx<n		................... is less than n
Xvx>n		................... is greater than n
X
Xx should be a letter: there are 26 variables, 'a' through 'z'. n should
Xbe a number between 0 and 100, variables are limited to these values.
X
Xln@m		Tests if item n is at location m
Xln!m		Tests if item n is not at location m
X
XIn these, n should be an item number, from 1 to 59, and m should be
Xa location number in the range 1 to 99. There is one special case for
Xthe item: item -1 refers to the player, so:
X
Xl-1@15
X
Xtests if the player is at location 15. Three special cases exist for the
Xlocation: -1 refers to the players inventory, -2 is the "out of the game"
Xlocation, and -3 is a synonym for the same place as the player. So
X
Xl23!-1
X
Xtests if item 23 is not in the players inventory, and
X
Xl42@-3
X
Xtests if item 42 is visible on the ground wherever the player is.
X
Xa-		Tests if the assistant is not with the player
Xa+		Tests if the assistant is with the player
Xg-		Tests if there is no guard nearby
Xg+		Tests if there is a guard nearby
X
Xrn		Tests if a random number from 0 to 99 is less than n - this
X		is handy for selecting from several random options.
X
XTo provide some examples:
X
Xif g+ r80	; this would test if there was a guard nearby, and an 80%
X		; chance succeeded
X
Xif l-1@5 l15@-1	; this would check if the player was at location 5, and
X		; carrying item 15
X
Xif ve=5 a-	; This would test if variable e had been set to 5, and the
X		; player's assistant was not present.
X
XNote that in the first case: 'if g+ r80' this could be followed by a simple
X'if g+' to catch the remaining 20% of chances when the guard was present.
X
XThe actions available are:
X
Xvx=n		; set variable x to n
Xvx+n		; add n to variable x
Xvx-n		; subtract n from variable x
X
XAs with tests, x is a letter between 'a' and 'z', and n is a number between
X0 and 100. Results are undefined if an addition or a subtraction take the
Xvariable value outside the range 0 to 100.
X
Xln=m
X
XThis makes location n be a copy of location m. The intended use of this is
Xfor altering locations, consider the following two locations.
X
Xl 10
X%"in a room with a door in the south wall"
Xnp15
Xsn42
X.
X
Xl 11
X%10
Xnp15
Xsx73
X.
X
XIf the player is at location 10, and tries to go south, string 42 will
Xbe printed, but he'll stay at location 10. However, the phrase 'unlock door'
Xmight cause the door to open, this would be done by:
X
X	l10=11
X
Xwhich causes location 11 to be overlaid onto location 10. All the entries
Xare the same, save for the south movement, whice becomes sx73, which means
Xthat location 10 now has a route south to location 73. Note how the
Xdescription for location 11 is %10, this causes it to be a duplicate
Xof the description for location 10. As was noted above, both locations
Xinvolved in this action must be in the range 1 to 99.
X
X
Xin=m
X
XThis does the same for items, and can be used to alter item descriptions,
Xmove them around, or do anything else needed. This is where items 45 - 59
Xcome into play, normally these are not accessible, but they can be copied
Xwith this action, thereby altering items that are visible.
X
Xa-		removes the assistant
Xa+		makes the assistant appear
Xg-		temporarily disables guards
Xg+		re-enables guards [1]
Xgk		kills a guard standing beside the player
X
X[1] It should be noted that guards are initially disabled, this allows
Xthe player to perform some set of actions before they start appearing,
Xsuch as taking some item, or some other action. If a guard is beside
Xthe player, and he does some action that includes a 'g-', the guard will
Xvanish, but will re-appear immediately if a 'g+' is done.
X
Xe	This is the game end - when this action is performed, the
X	game is over, and the player has won
X
Xb	This is a bad game end, meaning the player has lost
X
Xn	print "Nothing happens"
X
Xu	print "I don't understand"
X
Xf	Usually, if a phrase maps to both a special effect and a normal
X	action (such as 'take item'), if a set of actions are performed
X	because of the phrase, and the associated effect, then the
X	default action is not done. This action causes the default action
X	to be done, even though some special actions were done as well.
X	So as an example, a phrase like 'take something' could be trapped,
X	and the effect would be:
X
X	f 19
X	else		; do this all the time
X	then "52 f	; print a message, but pick up the object anyway.
X	.
X
X	Also note how the single line is forced to happen by starting
X	with an else.
X
X"n	print string n from the string table
X
Xcn	score n points
X
Xtn	Teleport to location n. Note that movement of this nature does not
X	invoke the 'm 1 2 3' type of actions, they are only for normal
X	movement.
END_OF_FILE
